shadowfetch-missions (5.0.1-1) umbra; urgency=medium

  * "database is busy" under heavy disk load (5.0.0 STRESS-01): no Store
    connection checkpoints the WAL on close any more
    (SQLITE_DBCONFIG_NO_CKPT_ON_CLOSE). The last close used to copy the WAL
    back into the database under an exclusive lock held across two fsyncs,
    so `show`, `list` and the desktop's reads ran out of their 10 s budget
    while the worker finished a step. SQLite's passive auto-checkpoint after
    a commit keeps the WAL bounded and blocks no one; short CLI commands
    also turn auto-checkpoint off, so no fsync lands on them after they
    commit. Durability is unchanged.
  * `cancel` saves the stop request as a small file in the private state
    directory first and waits at most 2 s for the database. When it is busy,
    cancel answers exit 0 with cancel_pending and a notice, and the worker
    records the ordinary hash-chained cancel from its next check. Once the
    request is saved, cancel's own commit does not wait on the disk
    (synchronous=NORMAL), and the worker deletes the request only after
    syncing the WAL. A stop that arrives after the run's last check is
    recorded with the outcome, or as the new event cancel-arrived-late, and
    is never dropped; a queued mission whose stop is not yet recorded is not
    started. If the request cannot be written, cancel writes directly as
    5.0.0 did. Only a regular file is read as a request.
  * Missions created in the same second run, and list, in the order they
    were created (created_at, then insertion order). 5.0.0 broke the tie on
    the random mission id.
  * `show` and `list` report a derived `hold` for a queued mission the review
    gate is holding: the blocking mission's id and title and a message. The
    worker gates on the same hold, so the reason shown and the rule enforced
    cannot disagree. Nothing new is written to the database.
  * Depends: python3 (>= 3.12), for Connection.setconfig; the image ships a
    newer Python. The shadowfetch-fireline floor moves to 5.0.1.
  * Reports version 5.0.1.

 -- Shadowfetch Project <signing@shadowfetch.com>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 23:15:00 -0400

shadowfetch-missions (5.0.0-1) umbra; urgency=medium

  * Retry read-only queries (mission get, list and events) on a transient
    "database is locked", up to six attempts with a 0.05-0.5 s backoff,
    instead of failing the call. Writes still fail loudly.
  * Depends on shadowfetch-fireline (>= 5.0.0), whose Firebreak reads the
    agent network setting.
  * debian/copyright: Source is github.com/Shadowfetchapps/shadowfetch-linux.

 -- Shadowfetch Project <signing@shadowfetch.com>  Mon, 28 Sep 2026 18:30:00 -0400

shadowfetch-missions (4.1.0-1) umbra; urgency=medium

  * BREAKING: a mission must name its provider when more than one installed
    provider can serve the capability. Where only one can, the choice is
    still made for you.
  * BREAKING: an approval now covers the mission's network destinations, so
    Scope carries egress_hosts. A mission whose declared egress widens after
    approval is refused; the attack that widened it previously reported that
    the destination change was neither prevented nor detected.
  * Add Claude and a local-model provider, for four alongside Codex and
    offline media. The local model bridge is packaged rather than left under
    tests, and capabilities() reads local_ai from the registry instead of
    the literal string "deferred".
  * A revoked approval can no longer start a mission. require_approval()
    re-read revoked_at under BEGIN IMMEDIATE and then released it, and the
    approval-used record and the mission's approval_id followed on two
    further connections, so a revoke landing in that gap was chained BEFORE
    the use -- the exact "granted, revoked, used" log the code's own comment
    claimed to prevent. All three steps are one transaction now: 20 of 300
    trials started before, 0 of 300 after.
  * The approval gate runs in the engine before the state transition, so
    there is no window in which an unapproved mission is running, and every
    entry point -- CLI, worker, desktop -- is covered because they all come
    through run_mission. Scope is recomputed at run time from the provider's
    declared ceiling, not from what the mission claimed when it was
    approved.
  * Model selection is the provider's decision; the engine bounds only the
    string.
  * The worker unit no longer names one provider's credential file, so a
    second provider's key reached nothing. Each provider's credentials are
    read from its own file.
  * PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL ran before busy_timeout, and SQLite does not run
    the busy handler for a journal-mode conversion, so 16 of 48 concurrent
    opens raised and 6 of 18 CLI invocations produced empty stdout and a
    traceback -- to a desktop that parses stdout as JSON.
  * The domain rows a receipt reprints as fact -- which provider ran, which
    executable, what the sandbox enforced, which credentials were granted,
    which tools ran under which policy decision, whether the tests passed,
    who reviewed it and what they decided -- are bound to the event that
    commits them, so they can no longer be rewritten while `audit verify`
    reports the chain intact.
  * The blast classifier can no longer be silenced by the thing it
    classifies: a mission appending two lines to its own .git/config made it
    report "no remote configured" while git remote -v showed the push
    remote. git is also no longer invoked as a bare name, and
    executable_config no longer misses credential.helper.
  * Replace the 1 Hz worker poll with inotify on the state directory.
    Measured on an empty queue: idle CPU 0.128% of a core to unmeasurable,
    2,880 write syscalls per 180s to 1, 225 MiB/hour of dirtied page cache
    to 0, and median wake latency 0.447s to 0.0003s. Without inotify it
    becomes a plain 30s sleep and says so once on stderr rather than looking
    event-driven.
  * Workspace locks are a hierarchy, not one file each: a workspace
    operation takes the global lock shared and its own exclusive, a
    whole-system operation takes the global exclusive. The first version
    stopped excluding anything while a worker held the global lock during
    recovery.
  * Reconciliation settles crashed tasks and sessions, not stale missions
    alone.
  * seal_policy could not seal a new provider at all: it never emitted
    executable_trust, which approve() refuses without.
  * The recorded argv is the argv that runs -- the namespace helper was
    missing from it -- and a refusal before launch is recorded instead of
    vanishing.
  * The receipt a person reads before accepting agent work named two
    enforced controls as decorative and named two fields absent from the
    list it claimed to explain; it is derived from that list now. The
    credential broker's "race-losable" framing is corrected in the shipped
    text: an attacker sharing the sandbox holds the same ticket and may
    simply ask first.

 -- Shadowfetch Project <signing@shadowfetch.com>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 18:28:58 -0400

